; η AT ALL FOUR UTF-8 WIDTHS, on the host that has no library to hide behind.
;
; `string-of` is η : Scalar → Scalar*, the free monoid's generator injection.
; Three hosts spell it with one call — `string(rune(c))`, `String.fromCodePoint`,
; `Character.toChars` — and windows has no string type at all, so its `string-of`
; is a hand-written UTF-8 encoder in an assembly template
; (targets/windows/strings.oro). Four cases, because UTF-8 is four cases, and
; nothing else in the corpus reaches past the first one: `render.oro` only ever
; asks for a decimal digit.
;
; prints: string
;
; skip: java
; skip: js
;
; TWO TARGETS, and that is a limitation of PRINTING rather than of the encoder.
; `System.out` on the JVM encodes in the platform charset, so a printed `é` is
; one byte on a Windows console and two on Go — which is why `string-escapes` is
; ASCII-only and says so. V8 is skipped for the same reason. What is left is the
; comparison that matters here: windows' hand-written encoder against Go's own
; `string(rune(c))`, byte for byte.

(export run)

; inputs: 0
; expect: Aé日🙂
(def run (fn (n)
  (concat (concat (string-of 65) (string-of 233))
          (concat (string-of 26085) (string-of 128578)))))
